.TH BNDSTEST 1
.SH NAME
bndstest \- test that array bounds errors raise a catchable exception
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bndstest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Bndstest
checks that indexing an array outside its bounds, indexing a nil array
and taking the head of an empty list each raise a Dis exception, and
that a
.B "{} exception {}"
block in the same stack frame catches it and execution resumes
afterwards.
It also checks that in-range indexing is undisturbed; without that a
guard that always raised would pass every other case.
.PP
Each case prints
.B ok
or
.BR FAIL ,
and the last line is a count.
.PP
.B "Run it under both"
.B -c0
.B "and"
.BR -c1 .
Interpreted, these exceptions come from
.BR libinterp/xec.c ;
compiled, the arm64 JIT raises them from its own emitted code in
.BR libinterp/comp-arm64.c .
.IP
.EX
emu -c0 /dis/sh.dis -c bndstest
emu -c1 /dis/sh.dis -c bndstest
.EE
.PP
The JIT used to reach its raising macro with
.B R.PC
left stale.
.IR Handler ()
derives the instruction offset from
.BR R.PC ,
so the offset fell outside every handler's
.RB [ pc1 ,
.BR pc2 )
range: the exception was raised but escaped the block that should have
caught it, and surfaced as a message from
.IR sh (1)
instead.
Under
.B -c1
this test failed at its first case.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/bndstest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR zdivtest (1),
.IR emu (1)
.SH BUGS
.B "THREE CASES STORE DELIBERATELY OUT OF BOUNDS."
Under
.I "emu -B"
or with
.B bflag
cleared (see
.IR sysctl )
nothing stops them, so they write wherever the index points.  One such
run on a Pi 3B wedged the board with
.B cpu0
in
.BR exit() ,
and the next identical run did not.  Running this test is the thing most
likely to happen right after the bounds checks are switched off, so the
hazard is the pair rather than either piece.
.SH HISTORY
.B "On a native kernel four cases USED TO fail, and it was a real defect,"
.B "not a test bug."
.B bflag
gates whether the JIT emits an array bounds check at all.
.B emu/port/main.c
has initialised it to 1 since forever;
.B os/inferno/port/dis.c
declared it
.RB ( "int bflag;" )
and nothing ever set it, so on every native kernel it was zero and the
compiled path indexed arrays unchecked \- an out-of-range store was
accepted and wrote wherever the index pointed.
.PP
.B "FIXED IN THIS TREE,"
and verified here on real silicon rather than under QEMU \- same board,
same card,
.B jit=1
read from
.BR /dev/jit :
.IP
.EX
build 1791422500 (bflag 0)   BNDSTEST 7 ok, 4 fail
build 1791424620 (bflag 1)   BNDSTEST 11 ok, 0 fail
.EE
.PP
.B "The failing arm's failures are themselves the proof the JIT was on,"
so that arm needs no separate flag check: the interpreter's
.B ind
instructions check unconditionally, so an interpreted run passes all
eleven whatever
.B bflag
says, and four failures are reachable only through compiled code.
.PP
.B "What the checks cost, on the board:"
.B array
is 6.5 per cent slower with them, ranges 3350-3480ms against
3080-3320ms, non-overlapping.
.B "The control is what makes that mean anything"
\- the
.B int
test indexes no array and shows 1.3 per cent with ranges that DO
overlap, which is what rules out drift and module reuse.  6.5 per cent
on the most index-heavy test in the suite is an upper bound, not a
typical cost.
.PP
.I "Emu -B"
suppresses the same checks and reproduces it exactly, message for
message, which is how this was attributed without touching the kernel
tree:
.IP
.EX
emu -B -c1 /dis/sh.dis -c bndstest
.EE
.PP
Under
.B -c0
it is 11/11 even with
.BR -B ,
because the interpreter's
.B ind
instructions check unconditionally. So this is a JIT-only hole, and a
kernel whose code generator is a stub is unaffected.
Setting
.B bflag
was a one-line change in the kernel tree, and
.B "it has been made here"
\- this page said "is not made here" for some hours after it stopped
being true.  A page asserting that nobody has fixed something is worse
than one that never mentioned it, and a status claim about another tree
ages the moment anyone acts on it.
.PP
Where it still applies: a kernel whose code generator is a stub is
unaffected, so a branch with no real generator is not exposed and has
nothing to fix.
.PP
Two cases accept either
.B "array bounds error"
or
.BR "dereference of nil" ,
because the two paths disagree and
.B doc/dis.ms
does not say which is correct: it states only that the operand must be
an array or a list of the correct type. The interpreter's
.B head
instructions do not test for
.B H
at all \- they dereference it and rely on the hosted signal handler
turning the fault into an exception \- while the JIT tests explicitly
and reports a bounds error. Whether the native kernels recover from
that dereference as the hosted emu does is untested here.
.PP
It does not cover the other JIT back ends, nor
.B IINDX
with a type whose size is not a power of two, nor a bounds error raised
in a frame other than the handler's: this build crashes outright when an
exception unwinds through a frame with no handler of its own, so a
deeper raise would test that defect instead.
.PP
It does not cover
.B slice
bounds,
.B indc
on a string, or
.BR tail .
